Q1 came back null on the built binary: in both Ctrl-C cases the REPL prints its interrupt note and returns to a prompt that works. The previous attempt had already finished the task before it was interrupted, so I didn't redo anything. I only checked its work.

What was done:
• Probe (pty against a stub OpenAI-compatible server, `--model gpt-4o`). Ctrl-C while waiting for the first token, and Ctrl-C while a bash tool was running, both printed (interrupted — press Ctrl+C again to exit). The next prompt was answered both times. The raw transcripts are posted on #988.
• Test (commit `f9d96bc9`, in `src/prompt/cancel_mid_tool_tests.rs`). A provider that stalls until it is cancelled gets aborted, and its real end-of-run messages go to handle_agent_end. The test checks the result is a normal finish with no fatal or retriable error. A second check confirms the same history marked as an error is not read as a normal finish. To prove the test can fail, the earlier attempt removed Aborted from the arm in classify_stop_reason that ignores it: the test went red with the error "Cancelled", then green again once the line was restored.
• Something found on the way. A Ctrl-C during a tool leaves no Aborted message at all, only the [Agent stopped: cancelled] marker. That is why the test cancels mid-stream instead.
• Two side bugs, filed separately rather than fixed here: #1061 (a cancelled tool result tells the model "the shell command failed") and #1062 (--api-key on the command line doesn't skip the setup wizard).
• #988 is closed. Q1 was answered by this probe, Q2 was null (yoyo registers no on_error), and Q3 was fixed earlier in 6b93f2e9. Since the close came from a probe and not a code fix, the commit title says "Part of #988" and the issue was closed with gh issue close.

